Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Flakhound**, a four-legged anti-air walker that strides
on legs, sweeps a back turret onto a bearing, and elevates twin flak barrels to track
the sky — as a 36x32x46 opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build
it by **painting discrete voxel cells** — one recorded operation at a time — and you
**author its walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Flakhound is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you
break the walker into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, target-tracking
anti-air platform needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation
at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the **authoritative
output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced another way (writing
files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the recorded
operations and the rig count.

`voxel-anim` paints **discrete opaque cells**: you set and clear single voxels, fill and
stroke boxes, draw 3D lines and spheres, and mirror across a plane — each voxel an
opaque `#rrggbb` color, with no transparency. Every operation targets a **part** with
`--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. The volume starts **empty**. After
each call the binary re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and
appends the operation to that part's log.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 5 --y0 5 --z0 4 --x1 30 --y1 20 --z1 40 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim fill-box --part turret --x0 12 --y0 20 --z0 12 --x1 23 --y1 28 --z1 28 --color "#565c64"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their keyframes), and
`voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the
authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's
progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`,
`scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole walker composed from
all your parts, re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm
the parts actually fit together and that the legs reach the ground. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished walker; the scene simply composes them there.

